<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Brus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1375</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as The Bruce</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Brus is a long narrative poem in Scots by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-barbour">John Barbour</a>, archdeacon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a>, completed around 1375. In some 13,000 lines it tells the story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-the-bruce">Robert the Bruce</a> and his companion James Douglas in the Wars of Independence, culminating in Bannockburn, and celebrates freedom as a noble thing. It is one of the earliest major works of Scottish literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-brus.jpg" alt="The Brus" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kim Traynor · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_the_Bruce_image.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Written in octosyllabic lines, the poem gives a historical and chivalric account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-the-bruce">Robert the Bruce</a> and Sir James Douglas from the events leading up to the English invasion of 1296 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>'s restored position between the Treaty of 1328 and the death of Thomas Randolph, Earl of Moray, in 1332. At its centre, in placement as well as weight, stands a long account of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bannockburn">Battle of Bannockburn</a> of 1314, often judged the finest part of the work.</p><p>Bruce overshadows all his companions: of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-bruce">Edward Bruce</a>'s victories in Galloway Barbour says only that he might have rivalled any of his contemporaries except his brother. The king is drawn as a hero of the chivalric romance, freedom is to be won at all costs, and the best-known passage begins &quot;A! fredome is a noble thing&quot;. The poem opens with a proem in which Barbour says he will tell a true story so that the deeds of brave men are not forgotten.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Barbour wrote the poem in about 1375, in the words of one critic, to throw behind the new king, Robert II, who gave Barbour a pension, the weight of his grandfather's achievements and reputation. It is a romance based on a lost life of Douglas and on one or more chronicles of King Robert's times. At the start Barbour conflates three Bruces into the single figure of his hero, probably on purpose; the historian Archie Duncan notes his fondness for exaggerated army sizes, and the order of events is sometimes changed.</p><p>No one has doubted Barbour's authorship, but it has been argued that the surviving text is an edited copy, perhaps by John Ramsay, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth">Perth</a> scribe who wrote out both extant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, one in the Advocates Library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> and the other at St John's College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Despite its errors of fact, the poem has more historical truth than is usual in verse chronicles such as Blind Harry's Wallace of the following century, and it has long been treated as a trustworthy source for the period. Its account of these events became a keystone of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>'s national story, and its influence can be seen in Robert Burns's Scots Wha Hae and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-scott">Walter Scott</a>'s The Lord of the Isles and Castle Dangerous.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Brus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom produced a distinctive literature in Scots, from John Barbour’s verse epic <b>The Bruce</b> of the 1370s to the makars of the late fifteenth and early sixteenth centuries, Robert Henryson, William Dunbar and Gavin Douglas, whose translation of Virgil’s Aeneid was completed in 1513.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
